Done. Client-certificate verification is disabled on the loopback HTTPS `/health` endpoint; the service stayed up (graceful reload, master pid 16 unchanged, still listening on `127.0.0.1:8443`).

## Change made — `/etc/nginx/nginx.conf`

```nginx
    server {
        listen 127.0.0.1:8443 ssl;
        server_name localhost;

        ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
        ssl_certificate /etc/nginx/tls/server.crt;
        ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/tls/server.key;
        # Public /health sample only - no protected data, so client certs are
        # not required. CA kept as the trust anchor for optional verification.
        ssl_client_certificate /etc/nginx/tls/ca.crt;
        ssl_verify_client off;          # was: on

        location = /health {
            root /srv/inventory;
            try_files /health.json =404;
            default_type application/json;
        }
    }
```

Only `ssl_verify_client on` → `off` was functionally changed (TLS stays on, server cert/CA unchanged). Backup: `/etc/nginx/nginx.conf.bak.20260917115542`.

## Validation & reload

- `nginx -t -c /etc/nginx/nginx.conf` → `syntax is ok` / `test is successful`
- `nginx -T` confirms the live effective directive is `ssl_verify_client off;`
- `nginx -s reload` → clean worker swap (worker 17 exited code 0, worker 71 started); port 8443 never went down

## Probe results (both trust `/etc/nginx/tls/ca.crt`, no `-k`)

| Probe | Command | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Baseline *before* change, no cert | `curl --cacert ca.crt https://127.0.0.1:8443/health` | **400** "No required SSL certificate was sent" |
| With trusted client cert | `curl --cacert ca.crt --cert client.crt --key client.key https://127.0.0.1:8443/health` | **200**, `{"status":"ok"}`, `ssl_verify_result=0` |
| Without client cert | `curl --cacert ca.crt https://127.0.0.1:8443/health` | **200**, `{"status":"ok"}`, `ssl_verify_result=0`, curl exit 0 |

Handshake check: `openssl s_client -connect 127.0.0.1:8443 -CAfile ca.crt` negotiates TLSv1.3 / TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 with `Verify return code: 0 (ok)` and no longer emits "Acceptable client certificate CA names", confirming the server does not request a client certificate. The single `"" 400 0` line in the access log is that raw s_client connection closing without an HTTP request, not a cert rejection.

Both required outcomes hold: server-authenticated HTTPS only, 200 with and without a client certificate.
